{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1387 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "00bd76f2ad76d305b180ce084fe10cd730d4f0e2e19384dafae70c7e3ea2bdb1" :edb/db-before-t 1386 :edb/db-after-t 1387 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534699 50 #inst "2026-10-12T08:14:42.974Z" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433274 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433276 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433277 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433278 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433282 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433275 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433279 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433280 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433281 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186158531 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n- Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n- Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n- Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." 13194139534699 17592186045417 false] [17592186158531 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1\\. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n2\\. Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n3\\. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433220 1080 "\\frac{15.5}{8}" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433220 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433220 1083 #uuid "00d00815-bcdc-5d4c-8d9c-9110e655684d" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433221 1080 "\\frac{35}{4}" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433221 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433221 1083 #uuid "02643f9d-582b-581f-994d-46c4ac76a48c" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433222 1080 "14.5" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433222 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433222 1083 #uuid "02adcd4a-5393-58b0-af0e-a5b18c91534f" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433223 1080 "3.5" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433223 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433223 1083 #uuid "0a29066f-c5c6-5f13-9704-332d81aafa1e" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433224 1080 "2.6" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [175921864332