an is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-21+19-15+31) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{4} \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433265 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1069 "q-143252" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ is a sample of size $n=100$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =35$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1072 #uuid "2b82cd64-97c1-5cdd-8cc8-36a5f17dbd56" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433275 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =35,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=35.$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433273 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1069 "q-149730" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n4,\\,19,\\,24,\\,3,\\,5,\\,20\n$$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1072 #uuid "2cf665ff-9d15-5f41-89fa-cbf6ac797c1c" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433276 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4+19+24+3+5+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{6} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433271 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1069 "q-88948" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n7,\\,-3,\\,-6,\\,9,\\,5\n$$" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1072 #uuid "9dd1476b-baa3-50d0-a7b4-7fe6d079ea16" 13194139534699 17592186045417 true] [17592186433277 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{